//
// A MOAT — the one hex whose surface is BELOW the ground around it.
//
// BACKLOG C5.  `docs/MATERIALS.md` § Defensive structures calls a moat
// *"a trench allowed to fill with water"* and names it the cheapest of
// the three because *"a moat is a trench plus the drop the palette
// already has"*.  This file is that drop, read.
//
// ── The invariant ───────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// > **A water hex sits `drop` metres BELOW the ground around it, and
// > every rule already in the tree reads that depth.**
//
// Nothing else here.  The trench is an ORDER like any other
// (`build.loft`), the trail that lays one is `play.loft`'s, and the
// waterline it becomes is `passable.loft::hex_ground`'s.  What was
// missing was the number, and `examples/palette.json` has carried it
// since plan 01: **sea 0, water 1, rapids 3, waterfall 8**, and until
// now nothing read it (`plans/25` § M2 open question 3 names the gap).
//
// ── ⚠⚠ WHAT WAS PROBED FIRST, AND WHAT IT KILLED ───────────────
//
// This file was designed around a claim that a probe FALSIFIED before a
// line of it shipped, and the corrected version is smaller and truer.
//
// **The wrong version**: *water already stops robots, so the depth is
// what stops the moat being free — it also stops the player's own crew,
// who HOVER.*  Every water kind carries `walk_vehicle: true`, the crew
// and the vehicle drive through `drive_along`, and `drive_along` asks
// `can_climb` — so a hovering mover looked like the one thing a depth
// could bite.
//
// ⚠⚠ **`walk_vehicle` IS READ BY NOTHING.**  `can_climb` refuses a step
// whose either end fails `hex_walkable`, and `hex_walkable` answers
// `walk_ground` for everybody — so **the player and the crew have never
// been able to enter water at all**, at any depth, boosting or not.
// Measured, not reasoned: a hover-climb drive east across a painted
// strip stops one hex SHORT of a `sea` tile whose height is 0.0
// (`tests/c5_the_moat.loft` § What the probe killed keeps the reading).
//
// So a moat is an absolute, symmetric barrier the day it is dug, and
// the depth changes nothing about who may cross an EMPTY one.  ⚠ The
// two consequences that survive are the two this file is for:
//
//   ⚠⚠ **what it takes to FILL** — `passable.loft::hex_ground` reads
//     the depth as its waterline, so a pile is a surface only once it
//     clears the water.  Without that the drop decides nothing: a
//     trench would flip to walkable rubble at the first grain of spoil
//     however deep it was.  ⚠ **This is the whole of what the number
//     buys**, and it is `docs/MATERIALS.md`'s *"a trench allowed to
//     fill with water"* said as arithmetic;
//   ⚠ **what it LOOKS like** — `ground_mesh.loft` draws a quad wherever
//     a hex stands above its neighbour, so a bank draws down into a
//     trench with no case written for holes.  A moat at 0.0 would be a
//     flat blue tile.
//
// ── ⚠ And the hazard the same probe exposed ─────────────────────
//
// ⚠⚠ **A player who rings themselves in a trench cannot get out — and
// unlike a wall, BOOST does not answer it.**  `docs/PLAYING.md` calls
// boost *"the only way out of a base you have sealed"*, and that is
// true of walls, whose tops are walkable; water is not a surface at any
// height, so a 3.0 m climb buys nothing against a 1 m ditch.
//
// That is recorded rather than guarded, and on the design's own
// grounds: `docs/GROUND_TYPES.md` § Entrances already makes *you MUST
// leave a gap, or accept what follows* the rule for walls, and `@X247`
// wants an irreversible decision at the moment the player knows least.
// A trench is that rule with teeth.  ⚠ It is the FIRST thing the player
// can build that they cannot cross back over, so `docs/PLAYING.md` says
// so in the row that offers it.
//
// ── Why `water` and only `water` may be dug ─────────────────────
//
// ⚠ `rapids` (3 m) and `waterfall` (8 m) are terrain a MAP AUTHOR
// paints and not orders the player may place.  The reason is the
// waterline above: a trench 8 m deep swallows sixteen bodies before it
// is ground again, which is not a barrier the game has any way to
// remove — where `water`'s 1 m is two.  ⚠ `build.loft::as_moat_kind`
// is where that lands, and `order_work_units` is what refuses the other
// two: the list of what may be built at all was already there.
//
// ── What this file deliberately does NOT do ─────────────────────
//
// ⚠ **It is not plan 02's solver.**  `docs/GROUND_TYPES.md` § The water
// sub-palette reads `drop` as CUMULATIVE along a drainage chain — a
// river's height ABOVE the sea, integrated upstream — and `plans/25`
// § M2 rightly calls that a decision about the whole simulation.  This
// is the LOCAL reading: one hex, its own drop, no chain and no
// neighbours.  The two agree wherever a chain is one hex long, which is
// what a moat is; a river remains flat until plan 02 says otherwise,
// and gains its profile by this term growing a chain rather than by
// anything here being replaced.
//
// ⚠⚠ **AND SOMETHING FILLS ONE — see § What a besieger shovels, at the
// foot of this file** (BACKLOG C9).  This paragraph read *nothing fills
// one yet, which is the honest limit of this* until 2026-08-28, and the
// limit it named was real: a moat has no HP, so `structure_breakable`
// is false, and nothing dies IN a trench because nothing can be in one,
// so the waterline rule was correct and **unexercised by the runtime**.
//
// ⚠ What closed it is not what this file predicted.  The BACKLOG row's
// own mechanism was falsified by a probe (`@X283`), the fill lives in
// the DESIRE SWEEP rather than in `wave_damage` alone, and the
// stalemate this paragraph called *not a stalemate the design has to
// fear* turned out to be worth closing for a better reason than the
// clock: **a besieger stopped at a trench has to stand at a fixed
// distance and dig**, which is the most shootable thing a robot can do
// (`@M060`).

use palette;
use painted;
use height;

// How many metres of depth one unit of palette `drop` is worth.
//
// ⚠⚠ **CHOSEN, and said to be chosen** — the rule `docs/PROGRESSION.md`
// § P2 states for any number a feature needs: check it exists first, and
// where it does not, choose it in the open.  `GROUND_TYPES.md` says the
// drop values are *"dimensionless and tunable"*, so the scale is this
// file's to pick.  **1.0 is picked against the one thing the depth
// decides — how much it takes to FILL** (§ WHAT WAS PROBED FIRST) —
// priced in bodies, at `damage.loft::BODY_HEIGHT_METRES` = 0.5 m each:
//
//   | drop | depth | bodies to clear the waterline |
//   |------|-------|-------------------------------|
//   | sea 0       | 0 m | ⚠ none — the world's flat default, so nothing moves |
//   | water 1     | 1 m | **2** — the trench the player may dig |
//   | rapids 3    | 3 m | 6 |
//   | waterfall 8 | 8 m | 16 — ⚠ authored terrain, and never an order |
//
// ⚠ Two is the number that makes the trench a real barrier and a
// removable one, and it is the same *one to two bodies* band that
// `passable.loft` § Why a robot climbs 2.0 m lands on for a body ramp
// onto a `wall` — the two mechanics agree about what a small pile is
// worth, which is why neither needed a constant of its own.
//
// ⚠ **The first row is the load-bearing one.**  Sea is the sparse
// world's implicit default — an unpainted hex IS sea (`passable.loft`
// § Unpainted is SEA) — so a scale of any size leaves every existing
// world at exactly the height it was.  That is what let this land
// without moving one of the 745 gate measurements.
pub const MOAT_METRES_PER_DROP: float = 1.0;

// How deep this ground type is, in metres — 0.0 for anything that is
// not water.
//
// ⚠ It takes the ENTRY rather than a palette and a hex, so that
// `passable.loft` can ask it with the entry it has already looked up
// and no consumer pays for a second lookup.  ⚠ It also keeps this file
// off `PaintedWorld` for the height rule, which is what lets
// `passable.loft` import it without a cycle.
//
// ⚠ **`?? 0` is load-bearing, exactly as `painted_height_of`'s
// `?? 0.0` is**: `drop` is `null` for every land type and every
// structure — `palette.json` writes it that way — and a null compares
// as TRUE against a climb, so an unguarded read would give the right
// answer for grass by accident.  Say the zero.
// Example: @DRY-128
pub fn moat_depth(e: const GroundType) -> float {
    (e.drop ?? 0) as float * MOAT_METRES_PER_DROP
}

// How deep the hex at (q, r) is, in metres — 0.0 on land, on a
// structure, and on the sea.
//
// ⚠ An index no palette entry answers for is 0.0 rather than a guess,
// the same miss value `painted_ground` chooses for the same reason.
pub fn moat_depth_at(pal: vector<GroundType>, pw: const PaintedWorld,
                     q: integer, r: integer) -> float {
    mda_i = lookup_painted(pw, q, r) as integer;
    if mda_i < 0 || mda_i >= len(pal) { return 0.0; }
    moat_depth(pal[mda_i] ?? GroundType {})
}

// Is there a moat at (q, r) — a hex sunk below the ground around it?
//
// ⚠ **The SEA is not one, and that is the whole distinction**: an
// unpainted world is sea at drop 0, so this is false everywhere nobody
// painted.  A predicate that answered *is this water* would be true for
// the entire map.
pub fn moat_at(pal: vector<GroundType>, pw: const PaintedWorld,
               q: integer, r: integer) -> boolean {
    moat_depth_at(pal, pw, q, r) > 0.0
}

// ─── ⚠⚠ What a besieger shovels (BACKLOG C9) ────────────────────
//
// § What this file deliberately does NOT do named *nothing fills one*
// as this file's honest limit, and this is the follow-on that closes it: **a trench a wave is standing at
// gets filled in, so a moat is a TIMER rather than a wall nothing can
// break.**
//
// ⚠⚠ **The BACKLOG row's stated mechanism was wrong and a probe said
// so** (`@X283`).  It read *"a moat has no HP, so a besieger's target
// lands nothing"* — which assumes a target is NAMED and then wasted.
// It is not: water fails `hex_walkable`, so a moat hex is not a node in
// the DESIRE field either, `flow_steps` never offers it, and
// `enemy_target` answers the enemy's own hex.  A branch at
// `wave_damage` alone would have been dead code
// (`tests/c9_the_fill.loft` § What the probe found keeps the reading).
//
// ⚠ **So the change is in the DESIRE SWEEP first**, and it is the rule
// that sweep was always following said out loud: *an obstacle the wave
// can REMOVE is passable in it.*  A wall is (break it) and the lifted
// climb is how that was expressed, because a wall's top is walkable
// and only the climb refused it.  A trench is too (fill it) and needs
// the NODE rule widened, because water is not a surface at any height.
// ⚠ The sea is not a moat, so the field still stops at the coast and
// `flow.loft`'s unbounded-plane argument is untouched.

// How much damage-to-a-wall one metre of trench is worth.
//
// ⚠ **DERIVED from `damage.loft::WALL_HP`, and the derivation is the
// design**: a trench hex costs a besieger the FULL 100, the braced
// figure a wall reaches only when it is closed into a ring.
//
// ⚠⚠ **A trench is a wall that cannot be UNBRACED.**  `damage.loft`
// § Bracing exists because a free-standing fence topples at its loose
// ends — a straight five-hex wall presents 60 HP in the middle and 30
// at the ends, and *"a fence UNZIPS from its ends"*.  A hole in the
// ground has no ends, no neighbours to lean on, and nothing to lose:
// dig a straight line and every hex of it is worth the full figure.
// That is what the player is buying for the ten helper-seconds
// `BUILD_MOAT_UNITS` charges — the same price as a wall, for a barrier
// whose weakest hex is its strongest.
//
// ⚠ It is written as a literal rather than as `WALL_HP`, and that is
// [loft#962]: a `const` initialised from a SIBLING MODULE's const,
// imported through `use dryopea;`, panics the compiler on both
// backends.  ⚠ `tests/c9_the_fill.loft` asserts the two are equal, so
// the derivation is gated rather than commented.
pub const MOAT_FILL_DAMAGE_PER_METRE: float = 100.0;

// The metres of spoil a besieger shovels for `damage` points of the
// bite it would otherwise have taken out of a wall.
//
// ⚠⚠ **One door, and no per-class table** — the rule
// `passable.loft` § ONE AI, per-class DATA states: a class's whole
// mechanical difference is what it does to a wall and how fast it
// arrives, and a third row here would have broken it.  A besieger digs
// with the tool it chews with, so the miner is the best digger and the
// scout is nearly useless at it for free, exactly as at a wall.
//
// ⚠ **It is metres per DAMAGE and never metres per depth**, which is
// what makes the drop the timer: a `rapids` trench at 3 m costs three
// times a `water` one and a `waterfall` sixteen bodies' worth of
// shovelling.  Scale the rate by the depth instead and every trench
// fills in the same time, and the palette's 1-3-8 would decide nothing
// again.
// Example: @DRY-134
pub fn moat_spoil_metres(damage: float) -> float {
    damage / MOAT_FILL_DAMAGE_PER_METRE
}

// Shovel `metres` of spoil into the trench at (q, r).  Answers whether
// there was a trench there to fill.
//
// ⚠ **Guarded on `moat_at` rather than trusted**, and the guard is the
// SEA: an unpainted world is sea everywhere, so an unguarded fill
// called with a target off the perimeter would raise the open water
// into a causeway.  A moat is a hex somebody dug.
//
// ⚠ It goes through `height_raise`, so this needs no rule of its own:
// the layer accumulates, the newest deposit names the pile, and
// `passable.loft::hex_ground` decides when the spoil clears the
// waterline and the hex becomes ground again.  ⚠ **Nothing caps it at
// the waterline** — it does not need to, because a filled trench is a
// step the wave takes, so `enemy_target` stops naming it on the tick
// after it crosses.
pub fn moat_fill(pal: vector<GroundType>, pw: const PaintedWorld, hl: HeightLayer,
                 q: integer, r: integer, metres: float) -> boolean {
    if metres <= 0.0 { return false; }
    if !moat_at(pal, pw, q, r) { return false; }
    height_raise(hl, q, r, metres, RUBBLE_SPOIL as u8? ?? 0);
    true
}

// Fold a tick's SHOVELLING into the world's rubble layer, and answer
// how many trenches took some.
//
// ⚠⚠ **This is why the fill is a two-step and not one write**, and it
// is `damage.loft::damage_resolve`'s shape for the same reason.
// `wave_damage` runs BEFORE the movers, off the world the tick started
// with, and `spawn.loft` § The consequences land LAST is the rule that
// makes a scripted run repeatable: *a hex raised mid-loop would change
// the world for the enemies the roster happened to visit later and not
// for the ones it visited first.*
//
// ⚠ A trench is exactly that hazard, and worse than a body is: it is
// what `enemy_target` READS.  Shovelling straight into `state.heights`
// would make the second besieger's target depend on whether the first
// one's spoil had just cleared the waterline — the roster's ORDER
// deciding the siege, which `ENEMY_MOVEMENT.md` § The tick resolves
// once names as the invariant everything else rests on.
//
// ⚠ So the tick's bites accumulate in a layer of their own and land
// here, with the deaths and the breaks — and the wave crosses on tick
// N+1, exactly as it walks over a body and routes through a breach on
// tick N+1.
pub fn moat_resolve(spoil: const HeightLayer, hl: HeightLayer) -> integer {
    mr_n = 0;
    for mr_c in spoil.cells {
        if mr_c.metres > 0.0 {
            height_raise(hl, mr_c.q, mr_c.r, mr_c.metres, RUBBLE_SPOIL as u8? ?? 0);
            mr_n += 1;
        }
    }
    mr_n
}

// How much MORE spoil the trench at (q, r) needs before it is ground
// again — 0.0 where there is no trench, or where one is already full.
//
// ⚠ The waterline is `hex_ground`'s threshold and this is the same
// arithmetic read forwards, for a caller that wants the REMAINING work
// rather than the verdict.  ⚠ **Nothing in `src/` calls it yet** — the
// runtime only ever needs the verdict — and it is here so that the one
// place the threshold is spelled out stays one place: a crew remark, a
// HUD reading or a `.keys` measurement that wanted *how much more* would
// otherwise write `depth - rise` inline and own a second copy of the
// rule.  `tests/c9_the_fill.loft` § What is left to fill is what keeps
// it honest against `hex_ground`.  ⚠ It is strictly MORE than the depth that
// clears it (`rise > depth`, not `>=`), so a trench filled to exactly
// its brim still wants a grain — which is what
// `tests/c5_the_moat.loft` measured as *two bodies fill it to the brim
// and no further*.
pub fn moat_left_to_fill(pal: vector<GroundType>, pw: const PaintedWorld,
                         hl: const HeightLayer, q: integer, r: integer) -> float {
    mlf_d = moat_depth_at(pal, pw, q, r);
    if mlf_d <= 0.0 { return 0.0; }
    max(mlf_d - height_rise(hl, q, r), 0.0)
}
